Mandatory Phase 5: Complete signal characterisation

There are three signal columns for every asset-date observation. Verify that each of the 20 assets has exactly:

[
(r_{i,t},s_{1,i,t},s_{2,i,t},s_{3,i,t})
]

and that wide-to-long conversion never pairs one asset’s signal with another asset’s return.

1. Signal mechanics and data quality

For each signal and asset, calculate:

* Number of unique values and evidence of rounding or discretisation
* Longest run of unchanged values
* Frequency with which the signal updates
* Cross-sectional dispersion each day
* Within-asset versus between-asset variance
* Missingness conditional on asset, date, return and volatility
* Whether missing or stale signals predict returns
* Whether signal scale or distribution changes over time
* Rolling 252-day mean, standard deviation and quantiles
* CUSUM and rolling-distribution structural-break diagnostics

Flag signals that are effectively constant, stale, rescaled, clipped or reconstructed differently across assets.

2. Investigate levels, changes and innovations

For every signal construct, without look-ahead:

[
s_{i,t},\quad
\Delta s_{i,t}=s_{i,t}-s_{i,t-1},
]

[
\Delta_5s_{i,t}=s_{i,t}-s_{i,t-5},
]

[
\text{innovation}{i,t}
=s{i,t}-\operatorname{EWMA}_{t-1}(s_i),
]

and rolling percentile rank, rolling z-score and daily cross-sectional rank.

Test whether future returns are predicted by:

* Signal level
* Daily change
* Five-day change
* Innovation
* Absolute signal
* Absolute signal change
* Signal crossing zero
* Entry into or exit from extreme quintiles

Do not assume the raw signal level is the correct predictor.

3. Separate cross-sectional and time-series information

Decompose each signal into:

[
s^{CS}{i,t}=s{i,t}-\frac{1}{20}\sum_j s_{j,t}
]

and

[
s^{TS}_{i,t}

\frac{s_{i,t}-\bar s_{i,t}^{\text{rolling}}}
{\sigma_{i,t}^{\text{rolling}}}.
]

Test separately whether the signal predicts:

1. Which assets outperform other assets on the same date
2. Whether an individual asset performs better than its own history
3. The equal-weight market return
4. Market-neutral returns
5. Returns after removing each asset’s rolling market beta

Report whether each signal is:

* Cross-sectional alpha
* Time-series alpha
* Market-timing information
* A mixture of these
* Not predictive

4. Test all economically relevant targets

For horizons:

[
h\in{1,2,3,5,10,20,40,60,120},
]

test prediction of:

[
r_{i,t+h},
\qquad
\sum_{j=1}^{h}r_{i,t+j},
\qquad
\sum_{j=1}^{h}\log(1+r_{i,t+j}),
]

as well as:

* Return sign
* Absolute return
* Squared return
* Future realised volatility
* Negative-return indicator
* 5% downside-tail indicator
* Future maximum drawdown over the horizon

This determines whether a signal predicts expected return, direction, volatility or downside risk. A signal that does not predict mean return may still be valuable for CAGR through risk scaling.

For overlapping (h)-day targets, use Newey-West inference with at least (h-1) lags and moving-block bootstrap confidence intervals.

5. Shape and calibration

For every signal transformation:

* Sort observations into quintiles
* Plot mean and median future return by quintile
* Plot positive-return probability by quintile
* Plot future volatility and downside loss by quintile
* Fit linear, piecewise-linear and monotonic isotonic relationships
* Test symmetry between positive and negative signal values
* Test whether only extreme values are useful
* Test whether signal strength is monotonically related to outcomes

With only 20 assets, do not use daily deciles as the primary test.

6. Redundancy and incremental information

For each signal:

1. Regress it on the other two signals using expanding-window estimation.
2. Define the residual as its orthogonal component.
3. Test the residual’s predictive value.
4. Calculate partial IC and incremental out-of-sample log-return improvement.
5. Perform PCA on the three signals to identify common and independent components.
6. Test whether combinations work because of diversification or genuinely new information.

Compare:

* Each signal alone
* All pairs
* All three
* Equal-weight combination
* Ridge combination
* Signals orthogonalised sequentially
* Common PCA component
* Residual PCA components

A signal is redundant if its standalone performance disappears after conditioning on the other signals.

7. Pooled versus asset-specific effects

Estimate:

[
r_{i,t+1}

\alpha_i+\beta s_{i,t}+\varepsilon_{i,t+1}
]

and asset-specific slopes:

[
r_{i,t+1}

\alpha_i+\beta_i s_{i,t}+\varepsilon_{i,t+1}.
]

Compare:

* One pooled coefficient
* Separate coefficients by asset
* Ridge-shrunk asset coefficients toward the pooled coefficient
* Leave-one-asset-out estimates

Report whether signal direction is common across assets or differs systematically. Do not allow a few assets to determine the pooled conclusion.

8. Conditional predictiveness

Test each signal conditional on:

* Trailing market return
* Trailing market volatility
* Cross-sectional dispersion
* Asset volatility
* Asset trend
* Signal magnitude
* Other signal values
* First versus second half of the sample
* Five consecutive 500-day blocks

Test only pre-specified pairwise interactions:

[
s_1s_2,\qquad s_1s_3,\qquad s_2s_3.
]

Do not perform unrestricted interaction or feature searches.

9. Placebo and falsification tests

Run at least 500 repetitions of:

* Permuting asset labels within each date
* Circularly shifting each signal through time within each asset
* Block-shuffling dates while preserving short-term dependence
* Testing the signal against past returns as a placebo target
* Delaying the signal by one and two days

The real result must exceed the placebo distribution and remain useful with a realistic one-day execution delay.

10. Economic implementation tests

For every potentially useful signal:

* Construct unit-gross long-short portfolios
* Test daily, 2-day, 5-day, 10-day and 20-day rebalancing
* Test overlapping holding-period portfolios
* Report turnover and position persistence
* Apply transaction costs of 0, 1, 2, 5 and 10 basis points
* Calculate break-even cost
* Apply position, asset and gross-exposure caps
* Report CAGR, mean log return and probability of wealth falling below zero
* Compare equal-weight, rank-weighted and proportional-signal portfolios
* Test one-day and two-day execution delays

Do not optimise leverage during signal discovery.

11. Data-mining control

Before inspecting the final 500 days:

* Define the primary transformation, horizons and tests
* Separate exploratory findings from confirmatory tests
* Apply Benjamini-Hochberg correction across the complete test family
* Use nested chronological validation for model selection
* Use stationary-bootstrap model-comparison tests across candidate specifications
* Record every specification tested

The final 500-day holdout may be evaluated exactly once.

12. Final required outputs

Produce one matrix for every signal and transformation containing:

* Predictive target
* Best horizon
* IC and confidence interval
* Out-of-sample IC
* Direction consistency
* Number of assets agreeing
* Stability across time blocks
* Incremental value after other signals
* Turnover
* Break-even cost
* Execution-delay robustness
* Holdout performance
* False-discovery-adjusted significance

Produce a final classification:

| Signal component | Return alpha | Volatility information | Downside information | Cross-sectional | Time-series | Incremental | Stable | Tradable | Decision |

Distinguish raw level, change, innovation, cross-sectional component and time-series component for each of signals 1, 2 and 3.

No signal should be retained merely because one transformation, asset, horizon or regime produced a high in-sample CAGR.